/**
 * Invocable action that lets Flow builders find an existing Contact by email address.
 *
 * - Returns exactly one Result per Request, in the same order as the requests.
 * - Matches emails case-insensitively and ignores leading/trailing whitespace in the input.
 * - Blank, null or unmatched emails give found = false and contactId = null.
 * - If several contacts share an email, the one with the earliest CreatedDate wins
 *   (Id is the tie-breaker, so the result is always the same).
 * - Uses a single SOQL query per invocation, so it is safe for batches of Flow interviews.
 * - Runs "with sharing", so only contacts the running user can see are considered.
 */
public with sharing class ContactLookupAction {

    @InvocableMethod(
        label='Find Contact by Email'
        description='Finds an existing contact by email address (case-insensitive). If several contacts share the email, returns the earliest created one.'
        category='Contact'
    )
    public static List<Result> find(List<Request> requests) {
        List<Result> results = new List<Result>();
        if (requests == null || requests.isEmpty()) {
            return results;
        }

        // Collect the distinct, normalized emails across all requests.
        Set<String> normalizedEmails = new Set<String>();
        for (Request req : requests) {
            String key = normalize(req == null ? null : req.email);
            if (key != null) {
                normalizedEmails.add(key);
            }
        }

        // One query for the whole batch. Email comparison in SOQL is case-insensitive.
        // Ordering by CreatedDate and then Id means the first contact seen for each email
        // is the earliest created one.
        Map<String, Id> contactIdByEmail = new Map<String, Id>();
        if (!normalizedEmails.isEmpty()) {
            for (Contact c : [
                SELECT Id, Email
                FROM Contact
                WHERE Email IN :normalizedEmails
                ORDER BY CreatedDate ASC, Id ASC
            ]) {
                String key = normalize(c.Email);
                if (key != null && !contactIdByEmail.containsKey(key)) {
                    contactIdByEmail.put(key, c.Id);
                }
            }
        }

        // Build one result per request, keeping the request order.
        for (Request req : requests) {
            String key = normalize(req == null ? null : req.email);
            Result res = new Result();
            if (key != null && contactIdByEmail.containsKey(key)) {
                res.contactId = contactIdByEmail.get(key);
                res.found = true;
            } else {
                res.contactId = null;
                res.found = false;
            }
            results.add(res);
        }
        return results;
    }

    /**
     * Trims and lower-cases an email. Returns null for null or blank values.
     */
    private static String normalize(String email) {
        if (String.isBlank(email)) {
            return null;
        }
        return email.trim().toLowerCase();
    }

    public class Request {
        @InvocableVariable(
            label='Email'
            description='Email address to search for. Case and leading/trailing spaces are ignored.'
            required=true
        )
        public String email;
    }

    public class Result {
        @InvocableVariable(
            label='Contact Id'
            description='Id of the matching contact (earliest created if several match), or null if none was found.'
        )
        public Id contactId;

        @InvocableVariable(
            label='Found'
            description='True if a contact with the email was found.'
        )
        public Boolean found;
    }
}
```

Notes on the design:

- **Single query:** the emails from all requests are trimmed, lower-cased, de-duplicated and then queried together in one SOQL statement. SOQL compares Email case-insensitively, and the map keys are also normalized.
- **Earliest contact wins:** results are ordered by `CreatedDate ASC, Id ASC`, and only the first contact seen for each email is kept.
- **Order and count:** results are built by walking the original request list, so there is exactly one result per request in the same order. Duplicate or null requests are handled too.
- **Sharing:** the class is `with sharing`, so it only finds contacts the running user can see. I did not use `WITH USER_MODE`, because a user without access to Contact or the Email field would then get an exception that breaks the Flow, rather than `found = false`. Only the Id is returned.

