/**
 * Service for onboarding new Accounts together with their Contacts.
 *
 * All records are written through a single fflib_SObjectUnitOfWork. It is committed once,
 * so the whole operation is atomic and bulk-safe: one insert per SObject type, no matter
 * how many records are registered.
 */
public with sharing class AccountOnboardingService {

    /**
     * Creates one Account per map key. For every last name in that key's list, it creates
     * one Contact linked to the new Account.
     *
     * @param contactLastNamesByAccountName Account name => list of Contact last names
     * @return the newly created Accounts with their Ids populated. Returns an empty list
     *         and writes nothing when the input is null or empty.
     */
    public static List<Account> createAccountsWithContacts(Map<String, List<String>> contactLastNamesByAccountName) {
        List<Account> newAccounts = new List<Account>();

        if (contactLastNamesByAccountName == null || contactLastNamesByAccountName.isEmpty()) {
            return newAccounts;
        }

        // Parent types must come before child types so the unit of work can resolve
        // the Contact.AccountId relationship during commit.
        fflib_SObjectUnitOfWork uow = new fflib_SObjectUnitOfWork(
            new List<Schema.SObjectType>{
                Account.SObjectType,
                Contact.SObjectType
            }
        );

        for (String accountName : contactLastNamesByAccountName.keySet()) {
            Account acc = new Account(Name = accountName);
            uow.registerNew(acc);
            newAccounts.add(acc);

            List<String> lastNames = contactLastNamesByAccountName.get(accountName);
            if (lastNames == null) {
                continue;
            }

            for (String lastName : lastNames) {
                Contact con = new Contact(LastName = lastName);
                // The unit of work sets AccountId after the Account is inserted.
                uow.registerNew(con, Contact.AccountId, acc);
            }
        }

        // One commit. Each SObject type is inserted in bulk inside a single transaction.
        uow.commitWork();

        // The registered Account instances now carry their Ids.
        return newAccounts;
    }
}
```

**Notes:**
- **Registration order:** The unit of work is built with `Account` first and `Contact` second. This is the parent-to-child order it needs to insert Accounts first. It then fills in each Contact's `AccountId` through `registerNew(record, relationshipField, parentRecord)`.
- **Single commit:** `commitWork()` is called once. It does one bulk insert per SObject type, so hundreds of Contacts cost only two DML statements. It also uses a savepoint, so any failure rolls back everything.
- **Empty input:** A null or empty map returns before the unit of work is created, so nothing is written.
- **Missing list:** If a map value is null, the Account is still created, just with no Contacts.
- **Returned records:** The returned list holds the same Account instances that were registered. After the commit they have their Ids populated. The list follows the map's iteration order.
- **Sharing:** The class is declared `with sharing`, so the running user's record access applies.

`OpportunityWinService` uses one unit of work. The opportunity updates are registered as dirty records. Each `Deal_Won__e` is registered with `registerPublishAfterSuccessTransaction`, so it is only published after the updates in `commitWork()` succeed. If saving fails, fflib rolls back to its savepoint, publishes nothing, and rethrows the exception to the caller. The `Application` unit-of-work factory now includes `Deal_Won__e.SObjectType`, because fflib only accepts event registrations for types it knows about.

