I'd do it differently. A nested loop is readable, but at your volumes it can fail.

**Why the nested loop is risky:** In the worst case, 3,000 leads × 3,000 contacts is 9 million comparisons. Each comparison also trims and lower-cases the emails, or compares strings case-insensitively. The worst case is common here: most event leads won't be existing contacts, so each of those scans the whole contact list. That much work runs into the 10-second synchronous Apex CPU limit. It also runs inside the import transaction, so everything else in that transaction shares the same CPU budget. A CPU timeout can't be caught, so the whole import would roll back.

**What I'd do instead:** Normalize each email once and build a `Map<String, Id>` from the contacts. Keep only the first contact for each email, so the "first in `contacts` order" rule still holds. Then do one map lookup per lead. That is about 6,000 cheap operations instead of millions, and it's still easy to read.

The method doesn't query or change any records. Null lists and null elements are handled safely. The result always has the same size and order as `leads`.

