```apex
/**
 * Links child Contacts to their parent Contacts using NPSP Relationships.
 *
 * Only the child-side relationship is inserted
 * (Contact = child, RelatedContact = parent, Type = 'Parent').
 * NPSP's relationship trigger, using the "List Setting" reciprocal method,
 * creates the reciprocal record automatically:
 *   Contact = parent, RelatedContact = child, Type = Son / Daughter / Child,
 * based on the Relationship Lookup for 'Parent' and the child's gender.
 * It also links the two records via npe4__ReciprocalRelationship__c.
 * The result is exactly one relationship record in each direction.
 */
public with sharing class FamilyService {

    @TestVisible
    private static final String TYPE_PARENT = 'Parent';
    @TestVisible
    private static final String STATUS_CURRENT = 'Current';

    public static void linkParents(Map<Id, Id> parentIdByChildId) {
        if (parentIdByChildId == null || parentIdByChildId.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Collect valid pairs and every contact Id involved.
        Set<Id> contactIds = new Set<Id>();
        Map<Id, Id> validPairs = new Map<Id, Id>();
        for (Id childId : parentIdByChildId.keySet()) {
            Id parentId = parentIdByChildId.get(childId);
            if (childId == null || parentId == null || childId == parentId) {
                continue;
            }
            validPairs.put(childId, parentId);
            contactIds.add(childId);
            contactIds.add(parentId);
        }
        if (validPairs.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // One query: every relationship among the involved contacts, in either direction.
        Set<String> existingPairKeys = new Set<String>();
        for (npe4__Relationship__c rel : [
            SELECT Id, npe4__Contact__c, npe4__RelatedContact__c
            FROM npe4__Relationship__c
            WHERE npe4__Contact__c IN :contactIds
              AND npe4__RelatedContact__c IN :contactIds
        ]) {
            existingPairKeys.add(pairKey(rel.npe4__Contact__c, rel.npe4__RelatedContact__c));
        }

        List<npe4__Relationship__c> toInsert = new List<npe4__Relationship__c>();
        Set<String> handledPairKeys = new Set<String>();
        for (Id childId : validPairs.keySet()) {
            Id parentId = validPairs.get(childId);
            String key = pairKey(childId, parentId);

            // Skip when any relationship already exists between the two contacts,
            // or when the same pair (in either direction) appears twice in this input.
            if (existingPairKeys.contains(key) || handledPairKeys.contains(key)) {
                continue;
            }
            handledPairKeys.add(key);

            toInsert.add(new npe4__Relationship__c(
                npe4__Contact__c        = childId,
                npe4__RelatedContact__c = parentId,
                npe4__Type__c           = TYPE_PARENT,
                npe4__Status__c         = STATUS_CURRENT
            ));
        }

        if (!toInsert.isEmpty()) {
            // One DML statement; NPSP creates the reciprocal records.
            Database.insert(toInsert, true, AccessLevel.USER_MODE);
        }
    }

    /**
     * Order-independent key for a pair of contacts, so A-B and B-A match.
     */
    private static String pairKey(Id a, Id b) {
        String s1 = String.valueOf(a);
        String s2 = String.valueOf(b);
        return s1 < s2 ? s1 + ':' + s2 : s2 + ':' + s1;
    }
}
```

**Other behaviour:**
- **Re-run safety:** a single query finds every relationship among the contacts in the batch, in either direction. Pairs with any existing link are skipped.
- **Pairs that would conflict:** the same two contacts appearing twice in one input (for example A→B and B→A) are only linked once. Self-links and null Ids are ignored.
- **Limits:** the method uses one SOQL query and one DML statement of its own, however many pairs (up to 200) are passed.
- **Security:** the class runs `with sharing`, and the insert runs in user mode, so the running user's object and field permissions apply.

The class extends `npsp.TDTM_Runnable` and has no Apex trigger. NPSP's dispatcher calls it only while its Trigger Handler record is active, so deactivating that record turns it off.

It runs no SOQL. Every field it needs is already on the trigger records in after-insert and after-update: `IsWon`, `Amount`, `CloseDate`, `OwnerId` and `npsp__Primary_Contact__c`. It uses no DML statements of its own either. It returns the Tasks in NPSP's `DmlWrapper`, and NPSP inserts them in one bulk operation after the handler finishes, as TDTM handlers normally do.

