/**
 * Moves books of business between reps.
 *
 * Account trigger actions (territory recalculation) are skipped for the ownership change
 * through the Trigger Actions Framework sObject-level transaction bypass. Contact trigger
 * actions run normally.
 *
 * Governor usage per call, whatever the volume: 1 SOQL query and at most 2 DML statements.
 */
public with sharing class AccountOwnershipService {

    /**
     * Sets OwnerId = newOwnerId on the given Accounts and on all of their Contacts.
     *
     * The DML is all-or-none. Exceptions propagate to the caller unchanged. After this
     * method returns or throws, Account automation behaves normally.
     *
     * @param accountIds Accounts to reassign (up to 200). Null ids are ignored.
     * @param newOwnerId Id of the new owning User
     */
    public static void reassign(Set<Id> accountIds, Id newOwnerId) {
        if (accountIds == null || accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }
        if (newOwnerId == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException('newOwnerId must not be null.');
        }

        // Defensive copy, so the caller's set is never changed.
        Set<Id> ids = new Set<Id>(accountIds);
        ids.remove(null);
        if (ids.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Build the Accounts in memory. No query is needed to set OwnerId.
        List<Account> accounts = new List<Account>();
        for (Id accountId : ids) {
            accounts.add(new Account(Id = accountId, OwnerId = newOwnerId));
        }

        // 1) Account ownership change, with every Account trigger action bypassed.
        //    The bypass covers only this DML. It is always cleared, even on failure.
        //    It is also cleared before the Contact DML, so any Account updates made by
        //    Contact automation still run Account actions normally.
        TriggerBase.bypass(Schema.Account.SObjectType);
        try {
            update accounts; // all-or-none
        } finally {
            TriggerBase.clearBypass(Schema.Account.SObjectType);
        }

        // 2) Contact ownership change. Contact trigger actions run normally.
        List<Contact> contacts = new List<Contact>();
        for (Contact c : [
            SELECT Id
            FROM Contact
            WHERE AccountId IN :ids
        ]) {
            contacts.add(new Contact(Id = c.Id, OwnerId = newOwnerId));
        }

        if (!contacts.isEmpty()) {
            update contacts; // all-or-none
        }
    }
}
```

Design notes:

- **Bypass scope:** `TriggerBase.bypass(Account.SObjectType)` stops every Account trigger action for the Account DML only.
  - It is cleared in a `finally` block, so Account automation is back to normal for the rest of the transaction even if the update throws.
  - It is also cleared *before* the Contact DML. That way, if any Contact action updates Accounts (for example rollups), those Account actions are not silently skipped.
- **Exceptions:** there is no `catch` block, so DML exceptions reach the caller unchanged. Plain `update` statements are all-or-none.
- **Limits:**
  - Accounts are built in memory by Id, so they need no query.
  - There is 1 SOQL query (Contacts) and at most 2 DML statements, regardless of volume.
- **Security:** the class is `with sharing`, so it only reassigns Contacts the running user can see.
- **Input handling:** the caller's set is copied, never changed, and null Ids are ignored. A null `newOwnerId` is rejected up front.

Ids are collected in a transaction-static set across all trigger chunks. The finalizer runs once at the end of each DML operation. It takes the set and clears it, then enqueues one job, or none if the set is empty.

