One known edge case remains. With partial-success DML (`allOrNone=false`), Salesforce rolls back and re-runs the triggers for the records that succeeded. Static variables are not reset during that retry. An audited opportunity's task would be rolled back, but its Id would still be in the set, so the retry would not recreate the task. This affects any static recursion guard, including TAF's own counters.

The query runs once per trigger execution in `TA_Contact_Queries.Service`, which is the framework's "avoid repeated queries" pattern. The two actions only read the shared map, so 200 contacts still means one Account query. If none of the new contacts has an Account, no query runs.

Two choices that go beyond the spec:

- **Field-level security handling:** The classes are `with sharing`, so the query respects record access. `Security.stripInaccessible` then removes any Account fields the running user can't read. Those fields are simply not copied, so the user can't learn Account data they can't see. The insert still succeeds; it is not blocked by an exception.
- **Lookup map always fresh:** The map is rebuilt on every before-insert execution. If no new contact has an Account, it is set to an empty map. This stops a later insert in the same transaction from using stale accounts.

